---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { parseAtlas } from '../../lib/content/atlas';
import { publicationPath } from '../../lib/site';
const cells = parseAtlas();
---
<BaseLayout title="Atlas — Constraint Grammar">
  <article class="reading-index">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Formal reference · 6 × 3 × 3</p>
      <h1>The 54-cell atlas</h1>
      <p class="lede">Each cell is one coordinate in the theory’s finite state-space: a Transformation Pattern, Completion Topology, and Persistence Mode jointly constrain a generated form and its canonical crystallization.</p>
      <p class="index-guidance">Read down the list to compare neighboring positions, or open any cell for its coordinate-forced sentence, manifestations, constraint strength, and link to the corresponding plain-language exposition.</p>
    </header>
    <aside class="coordinate-key" aria-label="How to read an atlas cell">
      <div><strong>Transformation</strong><span>What kind of change answers the opening pressure?</span></div>
      <div><strong>Topology</strong><span>What makes that transformation sufficient?</span></div>
      <div><strong>Persistence</strong><span>What kind of achieved reality remains?</span></div>
    </aside>
    <ol class="atlas-list">
      {cells.map((cell) => <li><article><header><span class="cell-number">Cell {String(cell.number).padStart(2,'0')}</span><h2><a href={publicationPath(`atlas/${cell.slug}/`)}>{cell.canonicalCrystallization}</a></h2></header><p>{cell.coordinateGeneratedForm}</p><dl><dt>Transformation</dt><dd>{cell.transformationPattern}</dd><dt>Topology</dt><dd>{cell.completionTopology}</dd><dt>Persistence</dt><dd>{cell.persistenceMode}</dd></dl><a class="text-link" href={publicationPath(`atlas/${cell.slug}/`)}>Inspect cell →</a></article></li>)}
    </ol>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
